    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90451,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90451.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "It would make it a crime. Right now the emoluments clause says that you can't receive a gift from a foreign leader, but there's no actual penalty. You can stop it. from happening or, you know, essentially, you know, go back and make it have to be returned. But we would actually make it a crime for the president, vice president, anyone in their family to receive a gift so that you can be criminally accountable. There's going to be so many bills, I think. There's going to be an era of reformation, you know, post-Trump, just as there was post-Nixon, as he exposes vulnerabilities in our democracy. We're going to have to have a cleanup crew to fix it. Do you anticipate some of these bills will get passed in the next two years? I think in the House, there's not a lot of courage in the Senate. They seem to be in deep, deep denial. It was really disappointing last week to see a statesman like Orrin Hatch basically just say, what do I care that Donald Trump wasn't directing these payoffs that violated campaign finance law? I think, you know, someone, you know, put that statement in front of him the next day and said, sir, this doesn't sound like you because you saw that he issued, you know, a correction to that and said that, you know, the rule of law is important. But it seems like with this president, so many of them have either just looked the other way or they've taken out, you know, a paddle and just are paddling in his same direction.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
