        "text": "Transparency is morally superior to gradual disclosure under these circumstances.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "A full disclosure will lead to less long-term reputational damage compared to piecemeal revelations.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The public values honesty and accountability more than strategic silence.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker holds moral authority to give advice on how institutions should respond to crises.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90451,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90451.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "No, I'm not. But I think a new Congress is going to be tougher on them, and I'm going to introduce in a new Congress the social media duty to report peace, which is the counterpart to the individual. But I also believe that when it comes to these social media companies, they can go a hell of a lot farther and just making sure the user has awareness, because ultimately we can write all the laws, we can to stop this, but if everyday users don't have awareness about where the sources of information are coming from, then we're in pretty muddy waters.",
