  {
    "episode_id": 90451,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90451.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 146,
    "turn_text": "And with not bad people, I try to remember that own experience of mine. And I believe deeply in second chances. And when I was in Baltimore, I followed David Simon's work. the writer from the Baltimore Sun and the writer of the Wire, and he had said that every person deserves a second act and we should all be able to write our own endings. And if I saw a young person who had screwed up and was worthy of being given a second chance and wasn't going to be a threat to anyone around them, I'd always do all I could to make sure that they weren't defined by that screw up. It was hard, because judges sometimes don't like those deals. Your colleagues sometimes will call you weak if they think that you're just letting people off. And I don't think I got it right every time. I know I didn't get it right every time. But I did see that there is a justice system that limits the potential, especially of young black men, more than any other race in America.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
