    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90451.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 178,
    "turn_text": "I wanted to run immediately, and that would have been foolish. And I had a mentor, the same one who got me the internship and directed me that way. He said, Eric, don't come in hot, relearn your city, apply for a commission. And I thought, OK, fine. I'll apply for the Planning Commission. He said, no, no, no. You're going to be on the Arts Commission. And I told him, I said, I don't know shit about art. What am I going to do on the Arts Commission? He said, well, you're going to learn how public art works and the heritage of the city. So after two years on the Arts Commission, I wanted to run for city council. And he said, no. Now you go in the planning commission. So I learned how planning in the city worked. And after that, ran for the city council. And I'm glad he did that. And I give advice to young people all the time when they ask if they should go home and run for Congress. I tell them, had I run for the city council in 2006, two things would have happened. I would have lost. But I also would have been so demoralized that I don't think I ever would have run for office again.",
